- question: Does the Fomalhaut debris disk host a forced eccentricity gradient with semi-major axis, and what planetary and formation scenarios are consistent with it?
- step 1: Fit stellar flux and position
  - method: Fit the star separately with a 10x10 pixel box mask [3.4.1 Fitting an EVD disk model: "masking all but a $10\times10$ pixel box"]
  - software: emcee, RADMC-3D
  - validation: Offsets consistent with zero [3.4.1 Fitting an EVD disk model: "the star is best-fitted with offsets consistent with 0.0 in both axes"]
- step 2: Fit eccentric-gradient disk model in image domain
  - method: RADMC-3D parametric optically thin disk with Gaussian semi-major axis profile and power-law forced eccentricity, convolved with the clean beam and fit to the primary-beam corrected image [2.2 Model setup: "by imaging 3-dimensional dust distributions within the `RADMC-3D` framework"]
  - software: RADMC-3D, emcee
  - parameter: 9 free parameters: M_disk, a0, w_r, e_f0, omega_f, n_pow, i, PA, h; 40 walkers, 6000 steps, first half removed [3.4.1 Fitting an EVD disk model: "fitted only for the disk mass ($M_{\rm{disk}}$), semi-major axis ($a_0$), disk width ($w_r$)"]
  - assumption: Dust sizes 0.9 um–1 cm, -3.5 power law, 3.5 g/cm3, optically thin, Kurucz stellar spectrum [2.2 Model setup: "fixed size distribution between $0.9\,\mu$m–$1.0\,$cm with a $-3.5$ power-law index"]
  - fitting: emcee affine-invariant ensemble sampler, Gaussian likelihood exp(-chi2/2) with pixel-correlation reweighting by l_pix^2/Omega_beam; priors not stated [3.3 Model fitting: "a Gaussian likelihood function ($\propto \exp({- \chi^2/2})$)"]
  - validation: Residual mean and std checked; autocorrelation time ~120, steps >50 tau; Verify fit to cycle 3-only data [Model convergence: "convergence is achieved when the number of steps exceeds ${>}50\times \tau$"]
- result: Forced eccentricity gradient index n_pow (General) = -1.75 ± 0.16 dimensionless [Table: "$n_{\rm{pow}}$ | – | $-1.75{\pm}0.16$"]
- result: Semi-major axis a0 = 138.79 ± 0.12 au [Table: "$a_0$ | au | $138.79{\pm}0.12$"]
- result: Gaussian width w_r = 13.51 ± 0.29 au [Table: "$w_r$ | au | $13.51{\pm}0.29$"]
- result: Forced eccentricity e_f0 = 12.56 ± 0.12 % [Table: "$e_{f,0}$ | \% | $12.56{\pm}0.12$"]
- result: Dust mass = 18.70e-2 ± 0.20e-2 Earth masses [Table: "$18.70{\pm}0.20$"]
- result: Inclination = 66.44 ± 0.09 deg [Table: "$i$ | deg | $66.44{\pm}0.09$"]
- result: Stellar flux at 1.32 mm = 0.74 ± 0.08 mJy [3.4.1 Fitting an EVD disk model: "$F_{\rm{\star}}{=}0.74{\pm}0.08\,$mJy"]
- result: n_pow from cycle-3-only Verify fit = -1.16 ± 0.16 dimensionless [Model verification: "$n_{\rm{pow}}{=}-1.16{\pm}0.16$"]
- limitation (authors): Massless-disk N-body ignores self-gravity; noise non-Gaussian near minor axis; residuals suggest width at apocenter not quite right [5.2 Additional physics: "We have thus far explored the possible origin of Fomalhaut’s disk eccentricity assuming a stationary planet perturbing a *massless* debris disk."]
- not stated (card): emcee priors and bounds for each parameter; initial walker positions; Chittidi et al. pipeline/CASA version and the image file itself; RADMC-3D settings such as photon count, grid and dust composition; how the mask and primary-beam response were computed numerically; REBOUND random seed and particle output details

